            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Rakesh's HRA sweet spot",
              "prompt": "Rakesh pays ₹60,000 a month rent and his basic is ₹14,00,000 a year. Mumbai is a 50% city. What HRA component gives him the maximum exemption with no taxable HRA?",
              "correctValue": 580000,
              "tolerance": 1,
              "unit": "₹",
              "explanation": "Annual rent ₹7,20,000 minus 10% of basic ₹1,40,000 = ₹5,80,000. The 50% of basic cap is ₹7,00,000, which is higher, so ₹5,80,000 is the sweet spot. Any HRA above that would be fully taxable.",
              "_key": "k157"
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "quiz",
              "title": "Whose lever is it?",
              "question": "Which single change would cost its owner the most tax?",
              "options": [
                "Aditi dropping her meal card and phone reimbursement",
                "Rakesh moving to the new regime",
                "Sameer taking his car as cash salary instead",
                "All three lose about the same"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 2,
              "explanation": "Sameer would pay ₹1,87,200 more. Rakesh would pay ₹55,280 more by switching regimes. Aditi would pay about ₹7,640, because marginal relief caps her tax at the amount by which she crosses ₹12,00,000, but she would lose her nil-tax position. The bigger your slab, the more a perquisite valued below its cost is worth.",
              "_key": "k158"
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "scenario",
              "title": "Your turn: build Neha's structure",
              "scenario": "Neha, 24, has just accepted her first job in Pune at ₹10,00,000 CTC. She lives with her parents and pays no rent, has no dependants, no loans and no investments beyond her PF. Her employer offers a flexi basket with meal card, LTA, phone reimbursement, books allowance and fuel reimbursement, plus optional corporate NPS. She wants to start investing and to keep her monthly cash as high as possible.",
              "prompt": "Which regime should Neha choose, how should she allocate her flexi basket, and what should she ignore?",
              "modelAnswer": "Regime: new. With no rent, no children and no investments, she has almost nothing to claim in the old regime, and her tax in the new regime is nil anyway: roughly ₹10,00,000 of gross salary minus employer PF and gratuity, minus the ₹75,000 standard deduction, leaves taxable income around ₹9,00,000, well under the ₹12,00,000 rebate limit. Flexi: take the meal card, because she eats on working days regardless, and phone reimbursement against her actual bill. Take fuel reimbursement only if she drives for work and can keep bills. Ignore LTA and books: both are fully taxable in the new regime. Employer NPS: her tax is already nil, so NPS saves her nothing today and locks money until 60. At her stage, liquid investing (an index fund SIP, an emergency fund) is a better use of the cash, and she can add employer NPS later when her taxable income crosses ₹12,00,000. What she should watch: her first big raise. The moment taxable income crosses ₹12,00,000, meal, phone and employer NPS become valuable, and she should revisit this whole plan.",
              "_key": "k159"
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "checklist",
              "title": "Build your own structure",
              "steps": [
                "Write down your CTC and sort every line into cash, benefits, employer contributions and provisions",
                "Work out your taxable income and tax under both regimes on this year's real numbers",
                "If you are in the old regime, set HRA at rent minus 10% of basic, within the city cap",
                "If you are in the new regime, take meal, phone and gift components, then employer NPS up to 14% of basic",
                "Check whether you are near ₹12,00,000 of taxable income, where every rupee removed saves about a rupee",
                "Keep employer PF, NPS and superannuation within ₹7,50,000 a year",
                "Declare your regime and claims in April, keep proofs, and submit them before the deadline",
                "Redo this in April every year, and after every raise, move or life change"
              ],
              "_key": "k160"
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "disclaimer",
              "text": "All three cases are illustrations using tax year 2026-27 rules, simplified to focus on salary structure. They ignore other income, surcharge where not applicable, and state-specific variations beyond professional tax. This lesson is for education and is not personalised tax, investment or financial advice.",
              "_key": "k161"
            },
            {
              "_type": "keyTakeaways",
              "points": [
                "The same rules produce very different structures: Aditi pays no tax, Rakesh saves ₹55,280 by staying in the old regime, and Sameer saves ₹1,87,200 through a company car.",
                "Take-home as a share of CTC falls as income rises: 80.8%, 69.5% and 49.1%. That is not a failure of structuring, it is slab rates and deliberate deferral.",
                "Rent and children decide the regime. Without them, the new regime almost always wins.",
                "Near ₹12,00,000 of taxable income, small tax-free benefits are worth far more than their face value.",
                "At high incomes, perquisites valued below their cost, such as a company car, and employer NPS do the heavy lifting.",
                "Rebuild your structure every April, and whenever your rent, family, city or income changes."
              ],
              "_key": "k162"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
